As I say, my history knowledge is
very fuzzy, but I have a vague...

00:02:10.545 --> 00:02:15.225
and the vague, I, I always feel by
the way, this gets me to reading, you

00:02:15.395 --> 00:02:20.205
know, Whenever we're told to read in
school, we're asked questions about

00:02:20.210 --> 00:02:25.785
our understanding and I always resist
these listening comprehension questions,

00:02:25.790 --> 00:02:27.525
or reading comprehension questions.

00:02:28.005 --> 00:02:31.365
I like having an imperfect
knowledge of what I read.

00:02:31.455 --> 00:02:36.645
If I enjoyed what I read, if I found
it interesting if I understood many

00:02:36.645 --> 00:02:40.485
parts of it, but didn't understand
some other parts and I can't remember

00:02:40.485 --> 00:02:44.565
the exact date of this or who did
what to whom, it doesn't matter.

00:02:44.595 --> 00:02:45.825
It was still enjoyable to me.

00:02:45.885 --> 00:02:51.945
I liked reading for enjoyment, but
starting back whenever it was 2,500,

00:02:51.945 --> 00:02:55.635
3000 years ago, when the first people
started writing for whatever purpose,

00:02:56.174 --> 00:03:02.535
uh, up until basically the popularization
of, uh, of, um, you know, printing

00:03:02.535 --> 00:03:08.355
in the 15th century, um, and even
thereafter, most people didn't read.

00:03:08.355 --> 00:03:13.575
So the brain, which is, you know, from
an evolutionary perspective has had

00:03:13.575 --> 00:03:19.430
a lot of experience at listening and
deciphering meaning from listening.Hasn't

00:03:19.455 --> 00:03:25.635
had very much experience or exposure
at, uh, dealing with writing.

00:03:25.845 --> 00:03:31.690
And it's quite amazing to me that you
can have lines on a piece of paper,

00:03:32.430 --> 00:03:34.440
uh, whatever the script may be.

00:03:34.440 --> 00:03:37.350
It doesn't matter whether it's the
Latin script or whether it's Chinese

00:03:37.350 --> 00:03:41.380
characters or whether it's the, uh,
you know, Persian script or Hangul.

00:03:41.400 --> 00:03:41.940
It doesn't matter.

00:03:41.940 --> 00:03:48.360
You've got lines that are converted by
the brain into meaning sometimes also in

00:03:48.360 --> 00:03:50.520
most cases, in fact, converted into sound.

00:03:50.700 --> 00:03:54.420
So the brain has to do this processing,
which, which is a tremendous feat

00:03:54.630 --> 00:03:57.990
for the brain to do, because it was
something that the brain was not, you

00:03:57.995 --> 00:04:03.360
know, prepared to do over the hundreds
of thousands of years that, that

00:04:03.365 --> 00:04:07.080
human beings and the predecesors have
been wandering around on the earth.

00:04:07.710 --> 00:04:13.490
And so I enjoy reading, uh, you know,
right now I'm reading a German book.

00:04:14.740 --> 00:04:18.000
A German book about the
Austro-Hungarian Empire, which is

00:04:18.000 --> 00:04:19.860
where my parents were born actually.

00:04:20.769 --> 00:04:25.260
And to, to sit in bed with a book,
even if I'm reading on my iPad, which

00:04:25.265 --> 00:04:28.170
I like to do, because I can look up the
odd word in German that I don't know.

00:04:28.950 --> 00:04:30.919
Uh, it's cozy.

00:04:30.935 --> 00:04:32.100
I don't want the sound.

00:04:32.190 --> 00:04:33.360
I don't want the audio.

00:04:33.540 --> 00:04:38.160
I just want the written word to connect
with that written word because the

00:04:38.160 --> 00:04:43.560
written word conjures up the meaning
in a different way from listening.

00:04:43.920 --> 00:04:47.850
I mean, in a way the
written text is a record.

00:04:48.120 --> 00:04:50.820
It's a record just as the
audio is a record, but it's

00:04:50.820 --> 00:04:52.110
a different kind of record.

00:04:52.140 --> 00:04:55.590
And I'm not a neuroscientist so I don't
know whether different parts of the

00:04:55.595 --> 00:04:59.610
brain process the writing, as opposed to
the audio to convert them into meaning.

00:05:00.030 --> 00:05:04.500
But I am convinced that doing a
lot of reading as well as listening

00:05:05.190 --> 00:05:07.230
helps in acquiring languages.

00:05:07.590 --> 00:05:11.849
I've said before, If I just hear the
sound of a new language, I can almost

00:05:11.849 --> 00:05:17.670
not make out the sound, but if I get a
phonetic version of that, and in the case

00:05:17.670 --> 00:05:21.870
of a non phonetic script, like Chinese
characters, I, I would use the opinion,

00:05:22.170 --> 00:05:24.780
but it helps to define that sound.

00:05:25.050 --> 00:05:29.520
And gradually I get better at hearing
that sound, but I rely on the written

00:05:29.520 --> 00:05:32.219
script in order to decipher the sound.

00:05:32.670 --> 00:05:37.800
Uh, and I, I think this is true of
everyone and we see how people very

00:05:37.800 --> 00:05:42.765
often are influenced in how they
pronounce a word in another script.

00:05:44.025 --> 00:05:49.485
If say, say using the Latin alphabet,
if someone is say check and they read

00:05:49.490 --> 00:05:53.355
something in English, or if they're a
Spanish speaker and they read something

00:05:53.355 --> 00:05:57.555
in English, they will be influenced by
how, what the letters mean, how they

00:05:57.555 --> 00:05:59.265
are pronounced in their native language.

00:05:59.325 --> 00:06:02.685
And that'll be reflected in how
they pronounce the new language.

00:06:02.685 --> 00:06:06.735
So they're obviously not relying
entirely on hearing the sound.

00:06:06.915 --> 00:06:11.595
They are very much
influenced by their habit.

00:06:12.180 --> 00:06:18.120
You know, their reading habits and
what the value of the, of those letters

00:06:18.120 --> 00:06:20.039
are in their own writing system.

00:06:20.610 --> 00:06:24.990
So reading is powerful in how it forms
our brain, how it helps us acquire

00:06:24.995 --> 00:06:28.185
new languages, and it's pleasant.

00:06:28.335 --> 00:06:29.655
It's a different experience.

00:06:29.655 --> 00:06:32.505
I can sit with a book and
read for two, three hours.

00:06:32.955 --> 00:06:36.405
I can't sit, just sit and
listen for two, three hours.

00:06:36.405 --> 00:06:37.395
I lose concentration.

00:06:37.395 --> 00:06:38.115
I get restless.

00:06:38.474 --> 00:06:40.635
If I'm in my car listening, that's fine.

00:06:40.844 --> 00:06:44.025
And the great advantage of listening
of course, is that it's so portable.

00:06:44.085 --> 00:06:46.245
You can be doing other
things while listening.

00:06:46.365 --> 00:06:49.305
You can sort of fade in and
out while listening, cuz you're

00:06:49.305 --> 00:06:50.534
also doing something else.

00:06:50.655 --> 00:06:51.585
You're kind of happy.

00:06:51.854 --> 00:06:53.565
Whereas when you're reading.

00:06:53.985 --> 00:06:56.445
If you fade out, you kind of have
to reread the sentence again,

00:06:56.445 --> 00:06:58.545
because you're kind of more focused.

00:06:58.755 --> 00:07:00.945
At least that's my own
experience, more focused on the

00:07:00.945 --> 00:07:02.505
meaning when you're reading.

00:07:02.865 --> 00:07:07.245
And so reading other than the
fact that it's the fastest way to

00:07:07.245 --> 00:07:08.625
acquire in acquire information.

00:07:08.625 --> 00:07:12.795
If I, if I need to instructions on how
to fix my, uh, coffee maker or something.

00:07:13.095 --> 00:07:17.535
Like, or if I look up, uh, something
on the internet, uh, having to do with

00:07:17.535 --> 00:07:23.445
the, I don't know, recharging my iPhone,
I prefer to read because it's faster.

00:07:23.565 --> 00:07:26.115
I can select, I can
jump from spot to spot.

00:07:26.115 --> 00:07:28.305
I can, I can get that
information more quickly.

00:07:28.425 --> 00:07:32.985
If I have to listen or watch
through a video, then I'm kind

00:07:32.985 --> 00:07:37.665
of at the mercy of whoever is
speaking or producing the video.

00:07:37.845 --> 00:07:39.900
Whereas if I'm reading,
I control where I go.

00:07:40.480 --> 00:07:44.849
Uh, similarly with a book, if I wanna
skip this chapter or jump ahead or

00:07:44.849 --> 00:07:49.200
behind, and I know exactly where I want
to go to, I can skim the pages and see

00:07:49.200 --> 00:07:50.789
those areas that are of interest to me.

00:07:51.239 --> 00:07:57.359
So reading is a, is, is quite a different
experience, but reading is powerful.

00:07:57.599 --> 00:08:02.309
I've said before I consider with a
new language, when I have read a whole

00:08:02.309 --> 00:08:08.040
book paper book, without a dictionary
without the iPad, from cover to cover.

00:08:08.040 --> 00:08:09.900
I consider that to be a major milestone.

00:08:10.110 --> 00:08:14.880
I am far from being able to do that
in Persian or Arabic, because I'm

00:08:14.880 --> 00:08:18.960
not comfortable enough with the
script, but, uh, even in Polish,

00:08:18.960 --> 00:08:22.170
which I don't speak well at all,
I can make my way through a book.

00:08:22.260 --> 00:08:23.430
I know enough words.

00:08:23.760 --> 00:08:25.710
And of course the writing
system is familiar to me.

00:08:26.160 --> 00:08:30.150
So, uh, an obstacle when we're learning
languages, where the writing system

00:08:30.150 --> 00:08:35.475
is different is that you can't make
up for, in my case, uh, 70 plus years

00:08:35.475 --> 00:08:38.955
of reading in the Latin script and
expect that a couple of years of

00:08:38.955 --> 00:08:42.855
reading in the, uh, in the Arabic
script is gonna give me the same

00:08:42.860 --> 00:08:45.225
degree of familiarity with that script.

00:08:45.615 --> 00:08:50.175
And yet I know that if I, if I
wanna progress in Persian or Arabic,

00:08:50.175 --> 00:08:52.455
I have to get better at reading.

00:08:52.695 --> 00:08:58.470
Reading is a major, you know,
again, source of enjoyment, but

00:08:58.470 --> 00:09:00.810
also a major learning activity.

00:09:01.170 --> 00:09:03.420
And some people say
they don't like to read.

00:09:04.920 --> 00:09:08.550
All I can say is try to develop
the habit, start reading

00:09:08.880 --> 00:09:10.560
